      "text": "The length of the diagonal connecting point A and point B in a rectangular prism with length \\( L \\), width \\( W \\), and height \\( H \\) can be found using the 3D Pythagorean theorem. The formula for the length of the diagonal \\( d \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{L^2 + W^2 + H^2} \\]\n\nFor this problem, the dimensions of the rectangular prism are \\( L = 10 \\) inches, \\( W = 20 \\) inches, and \\( H = 10 \\) inches. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{10^2 + 20^2 + 10^2} \\]\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{100 + 400 + 100} \\]\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{600} \\]\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{100 \\times 6} \\]\n\\[ d = 10 \\sqrt{6} \\]\n\nSo the length of the diagonal connecting point A and point B is \\( 10 \\sqrt{6} \\) inches. \n\nThe answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\n\\]",
