      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal of a rectangular prism (often referred to as the body diagonal), we first need to understand how to calculate the diagonal for a 2-dimensional rectangle and then extend that concept to a 3-dimensional object.\n\nFor a two-dimensional rectangle with dimensions \\( l \\) (length) and \\( w \\) (width), the formula for the diagonal is given by:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2}\n\\]\n\nNow, extending this concept into three dimensions means we need to consider the diagonal \\( d \\) from a point \\( p=(l, w, h) \\) to another point, where \\( l \\) is the length, \\( w \\) is the width, and \\( h \\) is the height of the rectangular prism.\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem in three dimensions:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}\n\\]\n\nHere, the given measurements are:\n\\[ l = 10 \\, \\text{inches} \\]\n\\[ w = 20 \\, \\text{inches} \\]\n\\[ h = 10 \\, \\text{inches} \\]\n\nSubstituting these values into our formula for \\( d \\):\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{10^2 + 20^2 + 10^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{100 + 400 + 100}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{600}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify \\(\\sqrt{600}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{600} = \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 150} = \\sqrt{4} \\cdot \\sqrt{150} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{150} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{25 \\cdot 6} = 2 \\cdot 5\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\nd = 2 \\times 5\\sqrt{6} = 10\\sqrt{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the body diagonal is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\n\\]",
